@dataService = http://localhost:4005
@shopService = http://localhost:4004

### 1. Read products directly from the protected data-service - a safe method, no CSRF needed
GET {{dataService}}/odata/v4/catalog/Products

### 2. Try to place an order directly, without a CSRF token -> rejected with 403 + "Required"
POST {{dataService}}/odata/v4/catalog/Orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product": "P01",
  "quantity": 2
}

### 3. Fetch a CSRF token + session cookie directly from data-service. The "x-csrf-token" and
### "set-cookie" response headers are picked up automatically by request 4 via request variables
### (see https://github.com/Huachao/vscode-restclient#request-variables) - no copy/paste needed.
# @name csrfFetch
GET {{dataService}}/odata/v4/catalog
x-csrf-token: Fetch

### 4. Place an order directly, now with the token/cookie from request 3 -> 201 Created
POST {{dataService}}/odata/v4/catalog/Orders
Content-Type: application/json
x-csrf-token: {{csrfFetch.response.headers.x-csrf-token}}
Cookie: {{csrfFetch.response.headers.set-cookie}}

{
  "product": "P01",
  "quantity": 2
}

### 5. Read products through shop-service - forwarded to data-service, still no CSRF needed
GET {{shopService}}/odata/v4/shop/Products

### 6. Place an order through shop-service. cds-csrf-cache injects the cached CSRF token itself -
### no token/cookie handling needed here at all, unlike request 3+4 against data-service directly.
### Run this a few times in a row and watch data-service's terminal: "issued a csrf token" is only
### logged once, not on every call - that's the cache at work.
POST {{shopService}}/odata/v4/shop/Orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product": "P01",
  "quantity": 2
}
